Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Riemannian Infrageometry", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Riemannian infrageometry measures and describes a discrete substrate \
represented by a graph ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["G", "TI"], "=", 
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["V", "TI"], ",", 
    StyleBox["E", "TI"]}], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "60fe77f4-9aef-4c67-9664-f07c345f567d"],
 ". Euclidean infrageometry, in contrast, is synthetic geometry: \
constructions with natural objects (points, segments, lines, circles) in the \
substrate."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["", "GuideFunctionsSection",
 CellID->1866139230,ExpressionUUID->"a0ea3657-4c83-4862-93c8-ecf80cebc774"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Classical geometry has infinitesimality: a segment can be subdivided \
infinitely many times, whatever the scale of the observer. A graph has no \
infinitely small part. Infrageometry aggregates instead. An observer scale ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["r", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "36839a29-4a57-4a4d-b7ac-70d4061489f6"],
 ", the number of steps the observer inspects at once, must be given before \
an object or a measurement makes sense."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"c121dee4-e683-45e8-a0f4-98dcfeadf505"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The claim of infrageometry is that limits of graphs are enough to capture \
the geometry of a surface, although a graph describes only points and a web \
of paths between them. This is close to Riemann's original approach. Riemann \
looked for the infinitesimal object that captures the essence of measuring \
the length of a curve, and found the line element ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
   SuperscriptBox[
    StyleBox["s", "TI"], "2"], "=", 
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], 
    StyleBox[
     RowBox[{"i", "j"}], "TI"]], 
   StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
   SuperscriptBox[
    StyleBox["x", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["i", "TI"]], 
   StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
   SuperscriptBox[
    StyleBox["x", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["j", "TI"]]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "b2df813c-760a-4472-915f-07a7641583ac"],
 "."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"fca346d2-b1eb-45b1-90ed-ce8f8a0c490a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In synthetic geometry on a graph a construction is not unique; it is \
multi-valued. Two vertices are in general joined by many shortest paths, and \
so they have many midpoints. We deal with this by enumerating the objects a \
construction gives (points in dimension 0, paths in dimension 1) and \
attaching to the construction its vertex density ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["\[Rho]", "TI"], ":", 
    StyleBox["V", "TI"], "\[Rule]", "{", "0"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\[Ellipsis]", "}"}]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "8edc5f55-fd16-461c-8333-ddd64cb36934"],
 ", where ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["\[Rho]", "TI"], 
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   StyleBox["v", "TI"], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "ce07172e-0861-4bb6-b891-5d02dd9f26b2"],
 " is the number of objects through the vertex ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["v", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "d1a13034-ae42-4569-acb8-eb9dde9ae20b"],
 ". Let graphs converge to a space, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox[
     StyleBox["V", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["n", "TI"]], ",", 
    SubscriptBox[
     StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["n", "TI"]], ",", 
    SubscriptBox[
     StyleBox["\[Mu]", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["n", "TI"]]}], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1], "\[Rule]", 
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["M", "TI"], ",", 
    StyleBox["d", "TI"], ",", 
    StyleBox["\[Mu]", "TI"]}], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "56bf6073-4691-4383-8bcc-0c9b23dd4e05"],
 ", in the Gromov-Hausdorff sense for metric measure spaces, with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
   StyleBox["n", "TI"]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "d77b8ca5-15c5-408c-b0c9-fd0b74368f7e"],
 " the rescaled path metric and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["\[Mu]", "TI"], 
   StyleBox["n", "TI"]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "1d343a35-aab1-48fe-8bc5-f906d6c057d6"],
 " the rescaled counting measure. We imagine that the normalized densities ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["\[Rho]", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "/", 
   UnderscriptBox["\[Sum]", 
    StyleBox["v", "TI"],
    LimitsPositioning->False], 
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["\[Rho]", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["n", "TI"]], 
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   StyleBox["v", "TI"], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "b9f10558-c9c0-4bbe-8f6d-2b2119cc6484"],
 " then converge weakly to the Dirac density of the corresponding object in \
the infinitesimal world, concentrated on a point or along a curve."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"982c2897-ecb2-4887-ae39-d6de2bf51b7b"],

Cell["\<\
The Riemannian branch measures the substrate instead of constructing on it. \
Its objects are the level sets of the distance from a vertex: the closed ball \
B_r(c) = {v : d(c, v) <= r} and the shell S_r(c) = {v : d(c, v) = r}, each an \
inert head, InfraBall and InfraShell, whose volumes InfraMeasurement reads \
and whose vertices FindInfraRepresentative gives; FindInfraShell computes the \
shell as a sorted vertex list. A geodesic is read at a scale: the observer \
sees s steps back, and a walk is a geodesic at infra-scale s when every \
window of s consecutive vertices together with the next one is a shortest \
path, so scale 1 is any walk and scale Infinity is a segment; \
FindInfraGeodesic grows them, and the \"Graph\" of InfraMeasurement[g, \
InfraSegment[p, q]] and SprayGraph hold every geodesic between two vertices, \
or from one vertex, as a directed acyclic graph whose directed paths are \
exactly the geodesics, built from the distance matrix without listing them. \
The measurements are counts: the ball volume V(r) = |B_r|, the shell area \
A(r) = V(r) - V(r - 1) and the tube volume T(s) = |{w : d(w, core) <= s}|, \
each under a chosen convention for its outermost layer. The log-difference \
quotient q(r) = d log V / d log r is affine in r(r + 1) over a middle window \
of radii, and a regression there reads the dimension as the intercept and the \
scalar curvature as the slope, Bishop-Gromov on a graph; the ball and the \
shell are two independent probes of the same pair, and the tube about a \
geodesic reads R + Ric(v, v) along its core. The metric tensor at p is the \
matrix d(p, u) / d(p, v) with u the foot of v on the interval I(p, w): on a \
substrate whose large-scale metric is round it tends to max(0, cos theta), \
the Gram matrix of the unit directions at p, and on a periodic tiling to the \
projection tensor of the polyhedral norm the path metric converges to. The \
Riemann tensor is not here yet. The ball, tube, cylinder, cone and sphere \
heads and the two geodesic graphs are on the Euclidean Infrageometry guide, \
the covering dimension and the ball intersection complex on the Infra \
Topology guide, the tangent and cotangent spaces on the Infra Analysis guide, \
and the Levi-Civita connection on the Infra Fiber Bundles guide.\
\>", "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"414659fb-9e45-483e-8194-5a7d2c6497d2"],

Cell["\t", "GuideDelimiter",ExpressionUUID->"dd08ac4b-e79b-4299-969d-6f8541ee10b5"],

